  "summary_text": "Kyle, Port Washington is running hot and outpacing its own baseline by 10%, but you have two operational headwinds to address\u2014one at Hicksville and one structural across both primary stores.\n\nStart with what's working: Port Washington yesterday delivered $546 per groomer against a trailing-30-day baseline of $494. That's real productivity lift, driven by 6 active groomers and elevated bather support (8.1 hours vs. your 5.8-day average). Year-to-date you're up 36% in revenue on 35% more appointments\u2014the unit is scaling cleanly. November MTD is tracking +1.8% versus DOW-adjusted expectation, which means calendar isn't helping or hurting; you're simply executing on plan.\n\nBut flag this: add-on attach at Port Washington collapsed to 39% from 67% week-over-week, while retail dropped 23%. This is upsell and front-of-house discipline slipping, not a capacity problem. Hicksville is the real concern. Yesterday was up 11% total, but that masks a harder picture: MTD revenue is down 21% and appointments down 18% versus October. When you DOW-adjust for calendar, Hicksville actually *outperformed* expectation by 24%\u2014meaning the miss is structural (fewer appointment slots available, fewer bookings). Productivity per groomer yesterday was $314 against a trailing-30-day baseline of $404, a 22% drop. Hicksville has zero bather hours scheduled and only 2.5 groomer-days per day versus October's 2.8. This is a capacity constraint masquerading as a performance miss.\n\nGlen Cove is not a primary driver, but note the pattern: zero groomer-days scheduled for the next 7 days.\n\n**Capacity & coverage:** Port Washington staffs 7 today and 6 tomorrow with 15 bather-hours both days; Hicksville 5 today and 5 tomorrow with zero bathers; next 7 days Port 36 groomer-days, Hicksville 22 groomer-days."
